title ALES148 — Lotus's stereo_trim_silence: the silence stripper carried into stereo, the suite's first STEREO EDIT built on the segmenter, two independent silences cut in one lockstep by the silence-intersection rule
prompt Read REMEMBER and the 1024-round itinerary + six-season double-seat; take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first; approve my own design rounds; make progress before checking in; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates; baton prin recur.
think First lap of this recur turn. Read REMEMBER (custody gates: seed force-push, provisioning, funds/keys, Keaton's Kumara, deep debride, collaborator-domain seating — none reached by local read-only software), the 1024-round itinerary, and the six-season double-seat. The live git edge is Season C, the Lotus creative suite (waymark ALES); ALES147 stereo_segment closed last turn and its own recommend named the ascending crux: ALES96 (trim_silence, the silence stripper), an EDIT built on the segmenter, carried into stereo. Confirmed by git log that ALES96 = trim_silence.rye. Crux-first and Lindy-first agree: the analysis corner's first reader turned back into the suite's first stereo edit that composes an analysis result.
think The honest wall: every stereo edit so far ran the proven mono edit twice and stayed balanced for free, because it wrote values (a gain, a clip, a rail) and never changed length. trim_silence CHANGES length, and ALES147 proved the two channels segment INDEPENDENTLY — their silences differ in count and boundary. Trimming each channel by its own silence would give the two speakers different lengths and tear the stereo image apart. So the rung cannot run the mono trimmer twice; it must decide ONE cut for BOTH.
think The rule chosen — the SILENCE INTERSECTION: segment both channels through ALES147 over one shared frame grid, mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in BOTH channels, cut exactly those frames from both in lockstep (back to front so earlier indices stay valid). A frame silent in only one channel is KEPT — a hard-left pan is real audio the right's silence must never delete; union would delete real content, intersection keeps every frame either speaker fills. This is the correct behaviour of a real stereo silence trimmer, and its whole honesty is the LOCKSTEP law: running mono trim per channel desyncs (left 8, right 12) where this holds both at 12.
think Implementation: segment_stereo up front (read-only, all validation, empty-clip BadFrame) — a refusal leaves both channels untouched; a bounded jointly-silent frame mask (max_clip bools, sized once) filled by a forward pass advancing one monotonic cursor per channel across its tiling run list; a backward pass cutting each marked frame from both channels through ALES2 cut, bounds asserted so neither cut can fault and half-trim; postcondition asserts both channels lost exactly the joint silence and leave equal length. The crux law is proven against a second, independent by-hand reference (frame verdicts read by run scan) across five frame lengths, four floors, three splits.
obs No custody gate reached — purely local, two bounded i16 Clips, a self-approved design round. This is the first stereo edit that had to THINK: the earlier lifts inherited balance for free, this one earns it. The honest lesson recorded: when a mono edit changes length AND the two channels read independently, the stereo lift is no longer run-mono-twice — it must compose the two independent reads into one shared decision (here the intersection) so the pair stays balanced. Witness GREEN (four laws), tame_style_check bans clean, width-check exit 0. No braces-in-format red this round (the ALES147 print trap was avoided — GREEN string is prose, no struct literal). Front door lotus/README.md synced (ALES148 roster entry, stamp 20260815.051802).
loom witness=ales_stereo_trim_silence_witness result=GREEN laws=4 channels=2 class=stereo-edit read=writes-by-cut form=lockstep-cut decision=silence-intersection cases=stereo-trim-law(5flen*4floor*3split)+preservation(left-silent-right-loud-kept)+lockstep(mono-desync-8v12-stereo-12)+degenerate(all-joint-silent-empty,no-joint-silent-unchanged)+atomicity(empty,ragged,inverted-band,illegal-floor) reds=0
file lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye ALES148 — stereo_trim_silence, the silence stripper lifted to stereo: the silence-intersection rule (cut only frames silent in BOTH channels) in one lockstep, the first stereo edit whose cut is decided rather than given; segments both channels through ALES147, cuts jointly-silent frames from both through ALES2 cut back-to-front, four laws with the lockstep law (mono trim desyncs where this holds balanced); StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole.
file tools/ales_stereo_trim_silence_witness.rish the witness — three-say (Language/Style/Lens), builds, runs selftest, asserts the GREEN ales-stereo-trim-silence line.
file active-designing/20260815-051802_fill-ales148-lotus-stereo-trim-silence.md the self-approved design read for this rung — the wall, the silence-intersection rule, the shape, the four laws.
file lotus/README.md front door synced — ALES148 roster entry on the Status line, stamp refreshed to 20260815.051802.
recommend keep-going the stereo edit corner now grows on the analysis readers; the next ascending Lotus crux is ALES97 (split_silence, the silence splitter — cut AT the silence keeping each non-silent region as its own take) carried into stereo, the join-law twin of this trimmer, or ALES98 (top_tail) — crux-first, agent-doable, no gate reached, witness GREEN, TAME bans clean.
